{"name":"Light Blue","hex":"#add8e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pale blue colour: sky blue or similar.","A member of the Cambridge rowing team."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light_blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Light blue","blocks":[{"text":"Light blue is a color or range of colors, typically a lightened shade with a hue between cyan and blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Light_blue_color.jpg/330px-Light_blue_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_blue","wikidata":"Q1602687"},{"title":"Light Blue (fragrance)","blocks":[{"text":"Light Blue is a fragrance line by Italian fashion designers Dolce & Gabbana which was launched in 2001 and has won numerous fragrance-industry awards. The men's version was released in 2007 and has also won awards, including the FiFi Awards in 2008."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_Blue_(fragrance)","wikidata":"Q3832257"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/D-OIND_looking_up.jpg/500px-D-OIND_looking_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/D-OIND_looking_up.jpg/1280px-D-OIND_looking_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/D-OIND_looking_up.jpg/500px-D-OIND_looking_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AD-OIND_looking_up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Pack_ice_of_Antartica_-_antarktisches_Packeis_-_3.jpg/500px-Pack_ice_of_Antartica_-_antarktisches_Packeis_-_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Pack_ice_of_Antartica_-_antarktisches_Packeis_-_3.jpg/1280px-Pack_ice_of_Antartica_-_antarktisches_Packeis_-_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Pack_ice_of_Antartica_-_antarktisches_Packeis_-_3.jpg/50